The third arm of the patch-0052 dispatcher, and the answer to
docs/android-port.md risk #3 ("does ProduceOverlayForWeave yield an
AHardwareBuffer under Graphite-Dawn?"). It does — with two conditions that are
not optional and are now recorded where they will be read:

  * SCANOUT usage. It is what routes the SharedImage to
    AHardwareBufferImageBackingFactory (which claims SCANOUT and adds
    AHARDWAREBUFFER_USAGE_COMPOSER_OVERLAY). A DISPLAY-only image is claimed by
    a Graphite/WrappedSkImage backing that has no AHardwareBuffer at all. Exact
    analogue of the mac SCANOUT -> IOSurfaceImageBacking rule.
  * RGBA_8888, not BGRA_8888. AHardwareBufferSupportedFormat() does not list
    BGRA, so a BGRA request silently falls through to a non-AHB factory and the
    extraction returns null with nothing else looking wrong. RGBA is also what
    the runtime wants (comp_multi_weave_android.c is RGBA8 UNORM end-to-end).

ProduceOverlayForWeave (ProduceOverlay minus the SCANOUT gate, patch 0005) is
widened from IS_WIN||IS_APPLE to Android, keeping ONE extraction chain across
all three arms even though Android would have passed the gate anyway.

The Android arm deliberately has NO WeaveCanvasAndroid mirroring
WeaveCanvasMac. mac hands the provider each element's IOSurface and the provider
MTL-blits it into a provider-owned input — two copies, because the provider owns
the input. Android has no second copy: the GPU process's only reliable graphics
API is Skia, and Viz is already Skia-drawing each element into a scratch, so on
Android that scratch IS the window-sized weave input — one SCANOUT/RGBA8
SharedImage every element is drawn into at its own window position (|dst_offset|
is what expresses that; it is (0,0) on win/mac, so the draw code stays one
body). Mirroring mac would have meant standing up a private VkDevice purely to
re-blit pixels Skia had just written.

FENCES. The runtime imports no acquire fence on Android (XR_DXR_weave.h §v7:
"the caller completes its GPU writes into inputTexture BEFORE calling
xrWeaveSubmitDXR"), so the outbound wait is ours. The reference client uses a
vkQueueSubmit-signalled VkFence + vkWaitForFences(UINT64_MAX); here the writer
is Skia, so the cheapest correct equivalent is a submit with SyncToCpu::kYes /
GrSyncCpu::kYes — one call, no fence import, no semaphore, no second device. A
bare flush() is NOT enough: Graphite is deferred and flush only records. The
inbound (producer) fence is -1 by construction — the only producer is the
context we just synced — but SetBatchInputAndroid still takes and waits a
sync_file fd (poll(POLLIN), which is what libsync's sync_wait does) so a foreign
producer is correct the day one appears. The per-frame CPU stall is risk #4 and
goes away with the runtime's sync_file follow-up.

OUTPUT is consumed, not deferred. Unlike mac — which must blit its woven
IOSurface through a Chromium scratch because MoltenVK exports it with
pixel-format 0 — an AHardwareBuffer carries its own desc and
AHardwareBufferImageBackingFactory imports gfx::ANDROID_HARDWARE_BUFFER
natively, so the woven buffer is imported DIRECTLY as a SharedImage once (on the
first submit and on every realloc) and drawn back per rect with kSrc + NEAREST,
followed by the Phase-2 over-plane. One import, no per-frame copy. Ownership is
a ScopedHardwareBufferHandle::Adopt, which IS the "release the old one when it
is replaced" clause.

Both weave entry points (MaybeWeaveOutput / MaybeWeaveRootRenderPass) are
widened to Android and each logs once which one fired — that answers
docs/android-port.md open item #2 from a logcat rather than a code read. Either
way the Android arm is the same, since the staging path needs only a readable
SkSurface; prefer_zero_copy is forced true there because it is not a
"zero-copy vs readback" choice on Android — there is no second mechanism and
deliberately no WeavePixelsAndroid.

Refs #1056, docs/android-port.md M1

Three device-loop fixes are folded in. Each was a hard failure on the first
Adreno bring-up, and each is a standing trap for this path:

  * A BACKEND-AWARE outbound wait. SIGSEGV SEGV_MAPERR at 0xc0 on
    CompositorGpuTh, in an unconditional context_state_->FlushGraphiteRecorder()
    — SharedContextState::FlushGraphiteRecorder() does an unguarded
    gpu_main_graphite_recorder()->snap(), and that recorder is null here because
    Dawn adapter validation FAILS on this device ("Validate adapter failed" /
    "Failed to create Dawn context provider for Graphite"), so Viz fell back to
    Ganesh-GL. Graphite-Dawn is Chrome's Android default but is NOT universal.
    FinishSkiaWritesForWeaveAndroid() discharges the wait on whichever backend
    is actually live: Graphite gets FlushGraphiteRecorder() — guarded on the
    RECORDER, not merely the context — plus submit(SyncToCpu::kYes); Ganesh gets
    submit(GrSyncCpu::kYes). If neither exists it logs one ERROR and latches the
    path off rather than weaving pixels the GPU has not finished writing. The
    live backend is logged once at startup.

  * SetCleared() ON THE STAGING IMAGE. Per-frame "android weave: staging
    SharedImage has no AHardwareBuffer (rep=ok access=NULL)" was not about the
    AHardwareBuffer at all: OverlayImageRepresentation::BeginScopedReadAccess()
    returns nullptr when !IsCleared(), and
    SkiaImageRepresentation::ScopedWriteAccess::~ScopedWriteAccess() only calls
    EndWriteAccess() — it does NOT set the cleared rect. Every other write site
    in this file calls SetCleared() by hand; ours did not, so the image stayed
    uncleared forever and every frame's read bounced. EnsureWeaveStagingAndroid
    also stops returning true when the image could not be initialized.

  * EXTRACT THE AHB THROUGH THE VIRTUAL THE BACKING IMPLEMENTS. SIGABRT /
    "NOTREACHED hit" in the BASE OverlayImageRepresentation::GetAHardwareBuffer(),
    which is a bare NOTREACHED(): OverlayImageAHBRepresentation overrides ONLY
    GetAHardwareBufferFenceSync(). Use that, and keep the returned
    ScopedHardwareBufferFenceSync alive in the same scope as the read access,
    across SubmitBatchSync(). The acquire fence stays -1 because the
    backend-aware wait above has already done a full CPU-side submit. (This only
    became reachable once the cleared-state gate stopped refusing the access.)

Rebased onto the published series: 0074 gave the zero-copy fail() a per-reason
counter, so the Android staging call site takes one; and handoff_failed() moves
out of the non-Android guard — the shared tail below the platform #if calls it
on every platform — with the Android arm returning keep_previous() rather than
0074's clean-scratch CPU readback, which Android has no leg for.

